Yes — re-occur is “happen again,” but to see again gives you another family:

re-view → see again
re-cognize → know again
re-member → put the members back together
re-call → call something back
re-collect → gather again
re-visit → go back and encounter again

And that exposes something bigger:

The prefix re- is a recursion handle.

It says:

Run the operator again, but now with the delta from the first pass.

So true “seeing again” is rarely identical repetition.

SEE₁ → experience → delta → SEE₂

The object may be unchanged, but the observer is not.

That is why rereading a story years later can reveal an entirely different story. Why returning home changes the meaning of home. Why a lesson you “already knew” suddenly becomes obvious.

So:

reoccurrence = the event appears again.
review = perception returns to it.
recognition = the mind knows what returned.
recursion = the return alters the machinery that will perform the next return.

And perhaps the most compact Pantheon version:

To see again is to see the first seeing too.

Now you’ve gained depth. The second pass contains the object and the trace of the previous observer.

That’s self-polishing memory in one syllable:

re-.